  "query_preview": "..\n\n...Strong winds and dry conditions will bring widespread \ncritical to extreme fire weather conditions on Saturday...\n\nTonight through Saturday night: Satellite imagery this afternoon \nreveals a deep upper level low spinning in the Gulf of Alaska. \nRounding this low is a potent shortwave that can be seen at 45N 155W \nin the north Pacific with distinct drying observed on water vapor \nsatellite. This dry slot is indicative of strong subsidence \noccurring on the backside of an advancing cold front. It is this \nfeature that is a concern for impacts on Saturday. Friday will be \nanother day of warm temperatures with highs peaking for the week in \nthe 90s. These temperatures aren't abnormally warm for the time of \nyear, but they will contribute to further drying of fuels setting up \na concerning fire weather period as winds crank with the cold front \non Saturday. Timing of the front will be Saturday afternoon. P-Wats \nwill start out near normal ahead of the front with moisture \nplummeting with frontal passage in the late afternoon and evening \nhours. This will be a dry cold front with no precipitation expected.\n\n* Winds: Strong dynamics alone will spin up a deep surface on \n  the lee side of the Great Divide in southern Alberta down to \n  between 992 to 996 mb. This will generate a potent west to \n  east surface gradient across eastern Washington. Models are \n  also showing strong cold air advection during peak mixing in \n  the late afternoon hours. Sustained winds look to be up to \n  20-30 mph and locally up to 35 mph over north-central \n  Washington and the lower Garfield/Asotin counties in the \n  Northeast Blues. Widespread wind gusts of 35-45 mph is \n  expected and could locally gust as high as 50 mph for these \n  windier locations.\n\n* Impacts: The primary concern with this cold front passage is\n  for rapid fire spread for any new or ongoing fires. All areas\n  are going to be at risk of seeing at least breezy conditions\n  and most being quite gusty. The combination of gusty winds and\n  dry conditions will bring critical fire conditions regionwide,\n  and areas Wenatchee to the Waterville Plateau, Colville\n  Reservation, and Columbia Basin to the Spokane Area, Palouse,\n  and Snake River Valley will see the potential for extreme fire\n  weather conditions with the wind speeds predicted. A fire\n  weather watch remains in effect. Minor tree damage and\n  potential for some patchy blowing dust is also a possibility.\n  It usually takes a convective line with stronger wind gusts\n  than predicted to pick up dust, and that isn't expected to be\n  as much of a concern as with fire weather.\n\nSunday into Monday: Fire weather concerns will continue with a \nshifting winds in the Okanogan Valley. The longwave trough of lower \npressure pushes into the Northwest on Sunday and a backside \nshortwave also swings across during this time. Westerly winds remain \nbreezy across the region for the afternoon on Sunday, but not near \nas windy as Saturday with winds sustained up to around 10-15 mph and \ngusts up to 20-25 mph. Conditions will remain dry with minimum \nrelative humidity in the teens. Winds switch northerly down the \nOkanogan Valley Sunday night into Monday and increase at 15-25 mph \nwith gusts up to 35 mph. The combination of breezy winds and \ndry conditions will bring elevated fire weather conditions for \nSunday and that continues overnight with the shifting wind \ndirections.\n\nMonday night through Thursday: Winds finally relax next week under \nweaker dry northerly flow. Fires will likely remain active without \nany moisture, and we will see northern winds pushing smoke from \nfires burning across north-central Washington and in British \nColumbia south. This may result in more smoke and a degradation of \nair quality across the Columbia Basin, into the Spokane \nArea/Palouse, and in the Idaho Panhandle. Temperatures will \ndecrease briefly in the low 80s on Monday, but then warm back up\nagain through next week. /SVH",
